<p>
As you all know by now, I <i>really</i> hate being spied on by web sites and other on-line entities. I&rsquo;m pretty sure this misbehavior won&rsquo;t be legal much longer but I&rsquo;m equally sure that the on-line advertising industry won&rsquo;t go gentle into that good night and will find ways to nibble around the edges of whatever laws get passed.
</p>
<p>
Happily, there&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.magiclasso.co/">Magic Lasso</a> which pretty much solves the problem for Safari users. That would be the end of the story except that I use XWidgets with mu4e to read HTML emails and <a href="https://github.com/emacs-elfeed/elfeed">Elfeed</a> entries through the wonderful <a href="https://github.com/fritzgrabo/elfeed-webkit">elfeed-webkit</a>. They work nicely but now Magic Lasso is no longer filtering their content so cookies and trackers are being accumulated. For the longest time I couldn&rsquo;t find where they were being stored. I finally stumbled on its location and discovered that it was a simple text file that could simply be deleted.
</p>
<p>
The problem was that I had to remember to do it and more specifically, where the file was located. I finally stopped being stupid and wrote a simple function to delete the file:
</p>
<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-elisp"><code>(<span style="color: #a020f0;">defun</span> <span style="color: #0000ff;">jcs-kill-cookies</span> ()
  <span style="color: #8b2252;">"Delete the cookies from Webkit"</span>
  (<span style="color: #a020f0;">interactive</span>)
  (delete-file <span style="color: #8b2252;">".emacs.d/url/cookies"</span>)
  (message <span style="color: #8b2252;">"Cookies deleted."</span>))
</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
It&rsquo;s almost too simple to bother showing.
</p>
<p>
While I was at it, I also called <code>jcs-kill-cookies</code> when I quit elfeed so I don&rsquo;t have to remember to do it manually. All of this is trivial, of course, but it&rsquo;s another example of sanding down your workflow to make it as simple as possible. Now things pretty much take care of themselves. I suppose I should figure out a way to delete cookies after email too but for now I&rsquo;m happy that the problem is mostly solved.</p>
